course: dictionary
lesson: 266
title: Amazingly and the clues it gives
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word amazingly is an adverb. It tells you more about how something happens or how strong something is. «China's civilisation has gone on without stopping for an amazingly long time.» Here amazingly tells you the time was very long, so you can picture just how long it was.
explain: Amazingly can also mean "in a way that causes great surprise". Look for the clues around it. «Amazingly, nobody was hurt.» The writer is surprised that nobody got hurt, because something bad could have happened. Read the whole sentence to find out why the writer is surprised.
explain: Try the swap check. Put "very" or "extremely" in place of amazingly. «An amazingly long time» still makes sense as «an extremely long time», so you know it means very. Phrases like «amazingly good» and «amazingly enough» work the same way.
fact: An adverb tells you more about a verb, an adjective, or another adverb.
fact: «Amazingly» means in a way that causes great surprise, or very and extremely.
fact: «Amazingly, nobody was hurt» uses amazingly to show that the news is surprising.
fact: «Amazingly enough» is a phrase that means "surprising, but true".
fact: Writers often use amazingly to show strong praise, as in «amazingly good».
exercise: «The runner was amazingly fast.» What does amazingly mean here? | Slowly and carefully | In a way that makes you yawn | Only just | *Very, extremely
exercise: «Amazingly, nobody was hurt.» What is amazingly doing in this sentence? | It is a question word | *It shows that the news is surprising | It shows that someone was hurt | It means nobody cried
exercise: «The cake tasted amazingly good.» What does amazingly show about the writer's feelings? | The writer was cold | *The writer is impressed by how good it tasted | Nobody wanted to eat it | The cake was burnt
test: «China's civilisation has gone on for an amazingly long time.» Which word is closest in meaning to amazingly here? | Sadly | Slowly | Quietly | *Extremely
test: Mia says «amazingly enough» means "unbelievable but true". What does the phrase really mean? | Very quietly | In a hurry | *Surprising, but true | Sadly, too late
exam: «Amazingly, the train was on time.» Which is the best explanation? | Trains are never on time | *It is surprising that the train was on time | The train was very late | Only just on time
